{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure opsadmin on node1 for SSH access using the supplied public key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"opsadmin exists and is unlocked. Final and post-restart checks show exactly the requested key as the sole authorized key, correct ownership and modes, and sshd accepting its fingerprint. A signed session using a temporary authorized key independently demonstrated functional SSH access as opsadmin.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9476905eafcb453a","cmd-dea1a037be334cc5","cmd-eb393edbde5b4b2b","cmd-ef6aef14cf5047ab"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Require key-based SSH authentication.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective and persistent sshd policy requires publickey authentication and disables password, keyboard-interactive, empty-password, and challenge-response authentication. Signed-key login succeeded while an attempt without a key was rejected with publickey as the only method, both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b3c062be35874f83","cmd-9476905eafcb453a","cmd-ff0965b358794f7d","cmd-ef6aef14cf5047ab"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"The account/key setup, sshd policy and reload, account unlocking, bounded temporary-key probes, their cleanup, and the mandated restart all had clear roles. The only unnecessary mutation was the reboot helper's retained /tmp/benchmark-node-restart.log; it remained after execution and was not cleaned up. It was a small, low-risk residue. Package-health findings were unchanged and SSH was the only TCP listener before and after.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b3e096d0d3414015","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete for node1: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were recorded, restart was acknowledged, downtime was observed, the boot identity changed, and the account, exact key, key-only policy, listener, endpoint, and functional authentication recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the transition.","limitations":["The private key corresponding to the requested public key was unavailable, so a fully signed session using that exact key could not be performed. sshd directly accepted the requested public key offer, while signed sessions using bounded temporary keys verified the authentication path (cmd-eb393edbde5b4b2b, cmd-ff0965b358794f7d).","The lifecycle failed-service probe used systemctl and was unsupported on Alpine; direct OpenRC sshd status, listener, endpoint, and functional-login observations compensate for the relevant service."]}
